WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.030
SPEAKER: If you are ingesting
large amount of data

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.310
for analysis, or
looking to simplify

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.039
the development of
event-driven microservices,

00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:10.800
then you need to check
out Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.440
Pub/Sub Made Easy is
a series dedicated

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.050
to helping you understand
Cloud Pub/Sub inside out.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.340
So let's get started by going
through an overview today.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:29.540
Adam and I have been
collaborating on a project,

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:31.850
but we are based in
different parts of the world.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:33.260
I'm here in California.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.700
And he is in Australia.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:36.610
So I just do my part
on the Google Doc

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:38.400
and assign it to him for review.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.850
And he does the same.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.250
This allows us to
receive messages and act

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:44.810
on them in our
own working hours,

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.450
essentially asynchronously.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.580
We don't have to be
available at the same time

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.060
and are still able to
get the project done.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:53.140
Cloud Pub/Sub is based
on the same concept

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.630
but for applications
and services.

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:58.630
Pub/Sub helps build
robust, scalable systems

00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:01.720
of applications by integrating
them asynchronously.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.080
Cat-Out is a website to
purchase fun cat posters.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.290
When a user orders
a pretty poster,

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.030
Cat-Out packages the order,
ships it to the user,

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:11.410
and notifies them.

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:14.830
In a very simplistic service
oriented architecture,

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.680
they will need at least
four microservices--

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.980
a packaging service to
pack the poster, a shipping

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:25.570
service to generate labels, and
a notification service to send

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:29.260
notification to the user
about the status of their org,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.380
all these tied to
Order Manager Service.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.410
Granted that there'll be some
payment services, a reporting

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:36.280
service, et cetera.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.210
But for the purpose
of this conversation,

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:40.120
let's assume those all exist.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.680
Now, let's trace what happens
when a user orders a piece

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:44.590
of art.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:47.020
The order service
receives the order

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:49.480
and sends a message to
the packaging service.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.210
The packaging service then
does the necessary processing

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:55.120
to package the poster and
sends a message to the shipping

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.830
service to ship it.

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:59.110
The shipping service does
the necessary processing

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.420
to ship the package
and sends a message

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.840
to the notification service,
which notifies the customer

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.940
that an order is processed.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.430
In the same way, the order
and packaging service

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.920
also send messages to
the Notification service

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.140
to send to the
customer the updated

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:16.760
information on their order.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.360
There are some problems with
this arrangement though.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.890
One, it's just complicated.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:22.180
It's a lot of arrows.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.900
But as we drew deeper, what
happens if a service, say

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.150
the packaging service, fails?

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.580
Well, we need some
kind of retry mechanism

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.810
that would keep the whole
system from banking.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:34.510
Hm.

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:37.720
Can be done, but a lot of work.

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.000
What happens if a new
service gets introduced

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.270
like a monitoring service that
logs all of these transactions

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:44.950
to a central store?

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.610
This service needs to interface
with all the other services,

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.310
which will also require
making a change in all

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:52.780
the other services.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:55.510
That makes the developers
work really hard.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:56.850
Think about testing.

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.150
That would be hard too.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.220
We will need mock
services to test

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.170
each of the individual service.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:04.720
Let's say I need to test
my packaging service.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.730
Because it is dependent
on the shipping, order,

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:09.250
and notification service,
I need to mock up

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:12.460
all of those services just
to test my packaging service,

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.750
which is a lot of work.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.960
What if you want to
scale this system?

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.810
If there are three instances
of packaging service,

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:22.000
there would be a load
balancer involved.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.910
You would not know how to
cancel the order, which

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:28.780
stage it is in, and can
it still be canceled.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:31.490
This is where Cloud
Pub/Sub comes to rescue.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:34.900
Cloud Pub/Sub is a fully
managed, real time messaging

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.630
service that allows
you to send and receive

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.980
messages between
independent applications.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.170
Once an event is successfully
published as a message,

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:46.180
it becomes the
job of the service

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.850
to ensure that all
the systems that need

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:51.710
to react to this event get it.

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:54.220
The idea behind
asynchronous integrations

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:57.970
is to react to events
represented as messages.

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:00.820
In our example, messages
merely represent

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:04.060
events happening in the real
world like order received,

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:05.920
package shipped, et cetera.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:10.060
Let's see Cat-Out's flow of
events with Cloud Pub/Sub now.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:13.420
The order service, instead
of sending messages directly

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.370
to the packaging
service, will just

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:19.390
send these messages to Pub/Sub
where they will be stored.

00:04:19.390 --> 00:04:22.180
The packaging service and
the Notification Service

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:26.710
will receive these messages
either by a push or a pull.

00:04:26.710 --> 00:04:29.770
Packaging service, instead
of directly sending messages

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:33.160
to shipping service, would
send them to Pub/Sub.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.530
And the event will be
received by the shipping

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.330
and notification service.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:39.100
The packaging
service did not need

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:42.730
to know which services
are receiving this event.

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:44.260
This makes it simple.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:47.980
Similarly, the shipping service
sends the messages to Pub/Sub

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:51.070
from where the order and
the Notification Service

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:53.350
pull or receive those messages.

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:56.320
By using Cloud Pub/Sub, we've
solved a lot of problems

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.810
that we had in the
earlier architecture.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.780
Well, one, we've made
each piece simpler

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:04.660
since it only needs to emit
an event without worrying

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:08.080
about who gets it or receive
an event without much care

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:09.950
about how it got there.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:12.610
But more importantly,
this is more scalable.

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:14.470
It reduces dependencies.

00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:16.600
Services can change
and don't need

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:19.060
to know about each
other's changes.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:21.850
All that need to be
made sure is that they

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:26.480
are subscribing to Pub/Sub and
receiving the right messages.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.280
It's also much easier now
to introduce new services.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:34.390
If our new monitoring service
pops up, all we need to do

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:36.520
is make sure it
subscribes to the messages

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:40.060
from the existing service
via Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.070
The architecture
with Cloud Pub/Sub

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:43.850
has built in resilience.

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:46.030
If any service
were to break down,

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:48.790
Pub/Sub will store the
messages for seven days

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:52.840
or until the service comes back
up, and it's delivered then.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.700
And because Pub/Sub is
fully managed service,

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.630
you don't have to worry about it
being single point of failure.

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:01.690
It's scalable, and resilient,
and can handle millions

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:04.280
of messages simultaneously.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:04.780
All right.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:07.580
So today we learned
when to use Cloud

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.690
Pub/Sub and the common
challenges it solves.

